    "2_the_hybrid_beat_the_pure_simulator": {
      "finding": "The hybrid BEAT the pure simulator on the joint (2.1432 against 2.1476), rather than trading joint quality away to buy the winner clause as the spec assumed it would. Imposing a better-calibrated winner marginal on the simulator's conditional structure improves the joint outright.",
      "why_it_matters": "The v3 spec wrote the hybrid as a FALLBACK -- a concession that gives up some of the simulator's joint quality in exchange for a winner marginal that clears its clause. The measurement says it is not a concession at all. The two factors of the joint are separable and are best supplied by different models: the blend knows who wins, the simulator knows how. That is a positive result about composition, not a compromise, and it is the shape a future candidate should start from.",
      "evidence": {
        "hybrid_joint_log_loss": 2.1432,
        "pure_simulator_joint_log_loss": 2.1476,
        "delta_hybrid_minus_pure": -0.0044,
        "calibrated_simulator_joint_log_loss": 2.1454,
        "pure_simulator_winner_delta_vs_incumbent": 0.0042,
        "calibrated_simulator_winner_delta_vs_incumbent": 0.0022,
        "hybrid_winner_delta_vs_incumbent": 0.0
      }
    },
    "3_the_d3_control_rules_out_the_arithmetic": {
      "finding": "The D3 control shows the blend's own three heads, pushed through the identical cell scorer, give exactly 2.2013 -- the same number its composed joint gives. So the hybrid's gain is not the cell layout, not the re-weighting arithmetic and not a scorer that happens to flatter distributions shaped like the simulator's. It is the simulator's conditional structure.",
      "why_it_matters": "Without this control the hybrid's joint improvement would be unattributable: it composes a new scorer, a new layout and a new model at once, and the first two are shared with the incumbent only if someone checks. The control is the check, and it costs one walk-forward run.",
      "evidence": {
